Twenty-eight-year-old Chef Found Dead in San Francisco Store
Twenty-eight-year-old chef who recently immigrated to San Francisco was a staunch of local commerce this week.
But the victim's brother says he doesn't have any idea who's going to hurt him, adding: "Finally, he's started to thrive."
The body found in Westfield Mall on Wednesday morning was identified as twenty-eight-year-old San Francisco Frank Galicia residents, the medical examiner's office said.
The San Francisco police department has said it was a forcible death and an investigation was underway.
Louis Galicia's homicide brother told ABC KGO in San Francisco that Frank, who had previously been an in-line chef in Boston, got his dream job as a Chef at Sons & Daughters restaurant six months ago.
Sons & Daughters restaurant spokeswoman said they were "shocked and devastated by his deaths."
"We have a small team that works as a closely related family. We'll miss him very much," the spokeswoman said.
At these difficult times, our thoughts and grief belong to Frank's family and friends.
Louis Galicia stated that Frank first stenched in dormitims, but that recently "it finally began to thrive."
"He found an apartment, he dated a girl," Louis Galicia told KGO.
Louis Galicia said he couldn't figure out who wanted to hurt his younger brother.
He had a good soul and a big heart.
When he wanted to connect with the family, he always cooked our food, he made us dinner," Louis Galicia said.
He's never wanted to participate in an affliction.
It was a brother who took things as they are.
“While everything else in the world is wrong, he was an uncut diamond that illuminated every day,” he said.
If you have any information, please call SFPD at 415-575-44.
Czech Railways has invested more than $31 billion in modernizing vehicles.
They have air-to-ircumferation and new toilets.
In the last six years, the state-owned carrier has invested more than CZK 31 billion in modernizing vehicles.
One-thousands of cars are currently equipped with air-conditioning, about half of the trains have a new system of enclosed toilets.
Amounts for modernization in individual years, with the exception of 2012, are gradually declining.
Czech Railways has invested more than CZK 31 billion in modernization of vehicles in the last six years.
One-thousands of cars are currently equipped with air-conditioning, about half of the trains have a new system of enclosed toilets.
Spent money was reflected in a decrease in the average age by two years for locomotives and separate units and by six years for classic wagons.
A larger share of investments was placed by the carrier in regional transport, where about 60 percent of these money amounted, 40 percent then the company invested in the renewal of vehicles for long-distance transport.
The average age of locomotives and units currently reaches 19 years.
However, modernized older vehicles are counted as completely new in this indicator.
If the modernization of the vehicles did not take into account, the average age of ČD truck vehicles would increase by ten years.
On the average, there are personal wagons slightly older, the average age of which, when accounting for modernizations, is 23 years.
Taking into account only the age from the date of manufacture is taken into account, the average age of these vehicles would amount to 33.4 years.
In recent years, the classic rigs composed of wagons and tow locomotives have been gradually disappearing and are being replaced by motor or electric units in recent years.
Between 2011 and 2015, the locomotive declined from 280 to 223 units, similarly the number of classic wagons fell from 2716 in 2011 to 2132 in the middle of this year.
The carrier mainly disposed of old-fuel vehicles equipped with leatherette seats and doors without the possibility of blocking the opening while driving.
From January-July, the carrier scrapped 110 of these vehicles.
From the investments that ČD are planning to carry out in ČD in the near future, it is possible to include, for example, the purchase of 50 wagons for a speed of up to 200 kilometers per hour, which the carrier plans to deploy on the international line Prague - Bratislava - Budapest, or a framework contract for the purchase of electric units for the domestic express track.
The contract envisages the consumption of up to 30 pieces of motor and 20 pieces of electrical units.
For a quarter of a year, the authorities have not been able to get rid of illegal advertising.
We are utterly helpless, they confess.
Already for a quarter of a year, the editors of the online newspaper Aktuálně.cz follow the story of a billboard in South Bohemia, which now attracts motorists to visit Lipno, where there is not only an interesting tourist walk in the treetops, as well as an Olympic park.
Highway administrators know they're standing black there, but they don't say they can't do anything about it.
Hundundreds, maybe thousands of such advertisements all over the country.
According to clerics, it is to blame a flaccid law, due to which it is not possible to remove illegal mobile billboards immediately.
There is a complicated bureaucratical circle at first.
In the meantime, however, the company will change its name and the office is short.
Or it is enough to postpone the ad by a few meters and the official process has to start again.
A quarter of a year ago, the Directorate of Roads and Motorways Admitanced: an advertising billboard stands on our land by the state road from České Budějovice to Lipno.
Even for three months they were not enough for officials to get rid of an unauthorised portable construction with a giant advertising poster.
It's as if anyone doesn't care that it's standing right next to the road, in a fifty-metre-long protection zone, where for the sake of safety it's not allowed.
Road administrators say they cannot simply cope with the billboard because of the leaky law.
Maybe load it up and take him away.
"We mustn't just afford this," says Nina Ledvinová, a spokeswoman for the Road and Motorholes Directorate, citing a complex bureaucratic wheel that must be preceded according to the law.
The case of this particular billboard, the fate of which the online daily Aktuálně.cz began to follow in detail in the spring, is not unique and indicates a general problem that the state and the authorities cannot deal with effectively and quickly.
Just in South Bohemia, the newsroom counted dozens of similar mobile advertisements located against the rules just next to the roadway or in its vicinity.
The same goes for other counties.
"We have a hard time for companies to remove handkerchiefs," confirms Jiří Klása, the head of the transport department of the České Budějovice Regional Authority, whose colleagues are addressing similar stories.
According to Klása, a leaching law that binds the clerks is to blame.
At first, he has a difficult time to address the owner of the billboard in order to put the ads away.
However, bosses of companies are often unrivalled, or they know a trick that Aktuálně.cz has already described in the past when it began to be interested in the issue in a more detailed way: it is enough to move the mobile stand with advertising by a few meters, and officials can throw the previous call to remove the black ad in the trash.
That's because the bureau has to determine exactly where the illegal billboard stands.
"If someone moves it and the coordinates suddenly do not confuse it, it is viewed as a new device and we have to contact the owner again," explains Jiří Klása.
The new law won't help.
The change could be brought by a new law, banning billboards on highways from September of next year and limiting them even on 1st class roads.
Critics point out that the rules for tarnishing illicit and easy-to-transmit ads will remain the same as before.
And the authorities won't be relieved.
Firms can continue to earn tens to hundreds of millions of crowns a year for renting illicit surfaces, and no one can afford them.
For example, a billboard appealing to Lipno belongs to Commerz Billboard.
Allegiances are well aware of her: the České Budějovice Municipality is currently conducting three administrative proceedings for black-built billboards.
The company faces a three-hundred-hundred-hundred-and-thousand-strong fine in every proceedings.
These are several advertisements, which without permission were placed in the protection zone of the 1st class road.
Proceedings have not yet been terminated, says Jaroslav Mráz, the head of the Transport Department of the Municipality of České Budějovice.
The ad that Aktuálně.cz isn’t interested in is yet to appear among these cases, but it’s most likely to be added.
According to ŘSD spokeswoman Nina Ledvinová, the company did not respond to repeated written appeals for removal, and the case will thus be put on a regional and then probably magistrate officials.
Commerz Billboard is one of those suspicious companies that cannot be contacted.
It did not take over the post office and abolished the official seat in Žižkov more than six years ago.
It doesn’t even be clear who’s behind it.
In the past, the company's mail went to the South Bohemian businessman Anton Fischer, who is famous for a sharp dispute with the head of the Czech representation of the Hyundai brand.
However, Fischer, whose company Euro Billboard is renting the areas from Comerz Billboard and offers them to advertisers, has in the past dismissed any connection.
This spring this spring, when ŘSD became interested in an advertisement for Lipno at Aktuálně.cz’s suggestion, Fischer had state-owned clerks send a letter to Commerz Billboard to his guesthouse, where the company is allegedly newly based.
Even from this address, however, no one has contacted the highway administrators to this day.
Futile struggle.
Similar silence or opaque changes in manbicles are not uncommon.
Several years a few years ago, we gradually fined two-million-crown companies.
However, it changed the name and ID and there was nothing to deal with.
The executor backed the case back, the ads continued, perhaps in other places.
We are quite helpless against such companies and their entrepreneurial practices.
It's like fighting windmills, one of the clerks admits on the condition of an anonymity.
So far, no one is addressing an amendment to the law in this regard.
Aktuálně.cz interviewed a few deputies interested in the problem.
So far, however, they have not developed any activity.
On the other hand, there were MPs who wanted to prolong the deadline for the preservation of billboards at major Czech roads.
The government has dismissed it.
Billboards are a very susceptible and somewhat schizophrenic issue not only for national and regional and municipal politicians.
On the one hand, on the other hand, they know about the societal demand for their limitations, on the other hand, they themselves need them because of advertising in election campaigns.
I don’t give the rather established impression that when something doesn’t work, it is necessary to change the laws.
Farmers will reave less grain this year, but more rapeseed and poppies, statisticians estimate.
