Russia and Turkey: An alliance of “uncombined”.
It was a gesture that ended in a crisis.
The leaders of Russia-Turkey reunited on Tuesday to shake hands and announce a formal end to an eight-month-long verbal war and economic sanctuation.
But while Vladimir Putin welcomed the Turkish opposing party in the sumptuous hall of St. Petersburg, I had the impression that Ankara wanted more of this audacity.
There's been a handshake, yes.
“Putin didn’t slew, and he barely radiated a warmth, even according to his measured standards.
Recep Tayyip Erdogan, on the other hand, has repeatedly spoken about his “expensive friend” Mr. Putin – five times, as one of the reports says.
I hadn't counted.
He also vowed that relations with Russia will not only go back to its pre-crisis level, but they would be even better.
The next day, one of the local newspapers wrote that Erdogan behaved as if nothing bad had ever ever happened.
In my opinion, his enthusiasm betrayed the exact opposite.
But the thriving refrigerant radiating from Putin has shown that the Russian leader hasn't forgotten anything.
The real cause of the crisis was the first thing he mentioned in his introductory rendition: The downing of a Russian fighter jet by Turkey on the Syrian border.
Moscow's responsibility was furious at the time.
Putin was handing out the wounds around him and accusing Ankara of staggering a knife in the overwhelm Moscow.
The scandal was all the greater because it had come from an imagined Friend.
Rebuild real trust will be difficult, perhaps even impossible.
U.S. public opinion has also changed since December.
State-supervised media for a month was waging a massive nationwide campaign against Ankara.
All of a sudden, it looked like the Turks were to blame for it.
More serious were accusations from top prosecutors that Erdogan's own family benefited from illegal oil trade in parts of Syria controlled by the so-called Islamic State.
He emphatically deniebrated this.
But in St. Petersburg came an official message that it is time to move on.
After all, this meeting only took place because Putin received an apology he demanded from President Erdogan.
Russia could also declare a victory.
For Ankara, the benefits of declazing a truce are unclear.
In the first place, Erdogan was almost deprived of power last month during a failed coup and therefore needs all his friends.
Recurregnized terrorist attacks in Turkey have visually rocked it.
There is also an economic motive.
Russia's sanctions had a severe impact - especially the abolition of charter flights, which are transported annually by several million Russian tourists to the Turkish coast.
This number has fallen by almost 90 per cent.
When it comes to Russia, tour operators and charter companies will certainly be relieved here if flights are reinstated.
They make money on Mediterranean tourism at the end of the season.
Even this week state television promised cheaper fruits and vegetables when imports of agricultural commodities from Turkey are allowed again.
"Tourists there, tomatoes back here," as the report proclaimed in Vedomosti newspaper.
However, the visit had another political value for Moscow.
Ankara is annoyed into the West for what he sees as a poor reaction to the coup attempt.
In addition, long-running animosity lengthy talks about joining the EU and Putin's accession - who is eager to take advantage of the cooling and tear Turkey's ties with the West.
The Russian leader certainly received bonus points when Ankara asked him to help the elected authorities after attempting a cougar.
You have to understand that for Moscow, which has a deeply rooted fear of regime change, it is a given.
So the summit in this pompouch, seafighting palace by Russia and Turkey has made it possible to show what one of the analysts has described to me as an “conciliation-incompatible alliance”: how the forces of two countries that feel to be rejected and exploited by the West.
However, despite the fact that they have publicly demonstrated mutual reconciliation, there are fundamental differences between these two countries.
The first is Syria, where Moscow recently took the role of a peace-creator, but where Russia and Turkey stand on the opposite sides.
