U.S. President Putin Releases Head of Sergei Ivanov's Presidential Office
On Friday, Russian President Vladimir Putin unexpectedly dismissed Sergei Ivanov as head of the presidential office.
Ivanov was one of Putin's closest collaborators for many years.
Sixty-three-year-old Ivanov has been appointed a special representative for ecology and transport.
The Kremlin's statement states that Putin "leaded Ivanov from his services to the head of the Russian presidential office," but there was no reason.
Its place will be taken over by Anton Vajno, who was Ivanov's deputy since 2012.
Forty-four-year-old Vajno was previously a diplomat.
He was born in the capital of Estonia of Tallinn in 1972, graduated at the prestigious State Institute of International Relations in Moscow (MGIMO) and worked at an embassies in Tokiya.
On the website (in Russian) it is stated that he was later the head of the presidential protocol and the presidential bureau.
