The Kremlin’s website quotes remarks addressed to Putin stating: “It’s true that in 2012 I’ve interviewed you to envoy this very challenging post, you could even say a troubled post for four years.
Apparently, I've been the head of the presidential office for four years and eight months.
Ivanov took the post in December 2011.
Previously, he served as Deputy Prime Minister and Minister of Disagreement.
He is a member of the Russian National Security Council and previously, like Putin, he has worked for the State Security (KGB).
In the late 90's, when Putin commanded the Federal Security Service of the Russian Federation (FSB), which replaced the KGB, Ivanov was appointed his deputy.
When Putin ascended to power, he appointed Ivanov as one of his five closest collaborators.
At some time, there was even speculation that Ivanov could become the successor of Putin at the head of the state after his second parliamentary term, because a third term would be uninstitutional for Putin.
However, this post was taken over by another close collaborator, Dmitry Medvedev.
Before returning to the presidency just three-and-a-a-half years later, Putin became prime minister.
