The United Nations sharply criticized Bulgaria for criminalizing refugees.
Zeid Ra'ad al-Hussein, UN Commissioner for Human Rights, on Thursday accused Bulgaria of systematically violating human rights by criminalizing refugees.
An official Bulgarian government's strategy "provokes serious concern that Bulgaria is not adhering international law," Zeid Ra'ad al-Hussein warned in a statement.
He pointed out that "it is particularly troubling to witness how important and influententive public personalities publicly express support for criminal armed militias who brazenly hold hunts for refugees on the border between Bulgaria and Turkey."
"One of the most serious problems is that almost all people who switch to Bulgarian territory in an irrevocable way are automatically arrested," Zeid stressed.
Worse, they are often prosecuted and imprisoned – for a year or a year or more – if they try to retire.
Their attempts to retire from the country are criminedified despite the fact that everyone under international law has the right to retire from any country, including their own.
Bulgaria deprivated 14,000 migrants this year, last year for the same period 21 000.
Pokémon Go - the pursuit of health and happiness that pays off
Fascinated to see how the world is changing.
A number of people over the last decade have laminated that teenagemakers and children no longer play outdoors and prefer to spend their free time before the screens.
Pokémon Go picks up people from the sofas and leads them to activity.
Spending time in front of the screens originally shrugged on the TV, later on video games and most recently on smart phones.
It's great to keep an eye that technology and outdoor activities aren't precluding.
Of course, I'm talking about the fashion that Pokémon Go, a character-based app that first appeared in video games in the late 90's (later a cartoon series, playing cards and more).
As well as many cultural phenomena, there has been a decline in interest before the launch of this new game at the beginning of July in the U.S., Australia and New Zealand.
Listing in other countries was then postponed due to a massive interest that led to overburdening of the servers.
Pokémon Go gained more users than Tinder in a few days and almost as many users as Twitter.
How does it work?
Briefly, gamers on a smart phone go out looking for Pokémon to track using GPS, and when they find them, they can “picture them” (through a screenshot on the phone) or “catch them”.
Mánia engulfed a generation that played the game at a younger age, lifting it from the seats and driving it out to find Pokémon.
There was a significant increase in the number of steps in the U.S. thanks to this game, which a "average" person takes every day.
Apple's Cardiogram, which tracks 35,000-steem users, recently told the Washington Post that within two days of launching the game, the number of people who exercised every day has jumped from 45 to 53 per cent.
The company doesn't have any information about who plays Pokémon Go - it sees only gross data on exercise levels.
Many public campaigns aspiring to “find 30” to “swimming off the couch” and “being active every day” have fallen on barren ground because most public health-focused messages are dull and intended for people who don’t look good.
However, the importance of exercise must not be underestimated.
Those practicing on a regular basis reduce the risk of cancer or depression.
Physical activity contributes to the control of weight, high blood pressure, diabetes and a long list of benefits, which we can't fully state here.
But the beauties of Pokémon Go is that people go out and do something they enjoy.
So you cover three pillars of health - movement, fresh air and the rays of the sun. And fun.
Some people even get new friends when chasing Pokémon.
Of course, there will also be those boring patrons who are looking for any pretext to be able to complain.
And yes, someone can walk into the door.
And what, like?
There was a breakthrough.
At the time, Smartphone applications, such as Fitbit, were able to monitor our activity, but they didn’t give us a reason to do something, as is the case with this game.
The nomination takes an eye and the interest in it begins to subside at a certain point.
However, thanks to the success of Pokémon, there will be other games that will combine applications and virtual reality with an activity in the real world and will help a number of people in the pursuit of activity, health and a better way.
