The drug for sixty kroners a day can reduce the dangers of a heart attack
Thousands of patients at risk of a heart attack will be able to take a drug that costs around sixty kroner (two pounds). Authorities recommend administering it to multiple patients for an extended period of time.
Ticagrelor blood precipitation drug reduces the risk that the seizure will occur again in people with a heart disease.
The drug is now administered for 12 months after having a heart attack in order to minimise the risk of a strophy or other seizure.
NHS watchdog NICE recommends that the drug be administered to humans for four years to further reduce the risk of cardiovascular problems.
But NHS watchdog NICE now recommends that it be administered to humans for four years in order to further reduce the dangers of cardiovascular problems.
Approximately 140 000 people suffer from a heart attack each year in England, and a quarter of them will suffer an additional attack or a heart attack.
Infarction and stroking are caused by the accumulation of fat, which on the walls of the artery forms a continuous layer.
If there is a secession of this layer, a blood clot can occur, blocking the access of blood to the heart and the development of a cardiac infarction.
If the clot is released, it can continue through the bloodstream, block the blood supply of blood to the brain and cause a cerebral stroke.
People who have already had a heart attack are at a higher risk of another seizure.
Ticgrelor, which is produced by the British company AstraZeneca, which is sold under the trade name Brilique, reduces the risk of clots becoming less likely.
It is recommended that patients receive 90 mg ticagrelor for a period of 12 months and then 60 mg with aspirin, twice a day for another three years.
Professor Carole Longson, director of the NICE Health Technology Assessment Center, said: "Despite the availability of effective secondary prereprecaution treatment, there are a quarter of patients who have had a heart attack and which will affect an additional heart attack or stroking - often with devastating consequences."
Fear of repetition can have a significant negative impact on the patient's quality of life.
It has been shown that Ticagrelor in combination with aspirin is effective in reducing the dangers of other heart attacks and stroke in people who have already suffered a heart attack.
We are delighted to expand the treatment options available to many thousands of people who will be able to benefit.
Since information on the effectiveness and safety of Ticagrelor - especially the risk of bleeding - is limited beyond three years, there is no recommendation in the suggestion of treatment above this time.
