Court in France Refused to Crash Dusk Refugee Camp
A court-martial in France has dismissed authorities' orders in Calais to demolish dozens of makeshift shops and restaurants in the Jungle camp, where thousands of refugees live.
During a court hearing this week in Calais, the authorities argued in Calais that the state should be able to repeal a total of 72 makeshift stores, where people inside the camp are selling goods from cigarettes to energy drinks, they offer hair cutting, in makeshift restaurants they sell tea for 50 cents or basic dishes for a few euros.
Calais prefect's office has argued that places need to be demolished because they represent a parallel economy that doesn't pay taxes, and because they pose a risk of fire and health risks.
Humanitarian organizations in court have argued that shops and restaurants are vitary, and that free meals offered by state-sponsored associations and other groups do not provide an addendum to food for increasing numbers of refugees in the camp.
Disputable makeshift shops and restaurants often provide shelter and free food to people in need.
Lille court ruled that there is no legal basis that prevents people from running 72 temporary shops, but stated that the Calais prefect’s objections were “completely understandable.”
Judge Jean-François Molla said grocery stores, cafes and restaurants had a much greater role than simply serving food to people "lived in extraordinarily desperate conditions."
He said that the premises provide peaceful places to meet refugees and volunteers.
One of the places threatened with demolition was a cafe in the camp, Jungle Book Kids, operated by a non-non-non-non-non-non-profit organization that offers 200 meals every day, English and French lessons, and asylum counseling for hundreds of unaccompanied unaccompanied unaccompanied minors every day.
More than 170 000 people have signed a petition for the protection of the “children’s” café.
The  Guardian has received a letter stating that the closure of the café "it would be a catastrophe for these children, some of them are only 8 years old."
Charitative organizations Help Refugess and L'Auberge des Migrants on Friday estimated the number of refugees in the camp after a steep surge of arrivals during the summer rose to more than 9,000 - the highest number so far.
