We Knew it was going to happen, but it wasn't quite typical and it probably won't be my favorite time to play.
How do you evaluate the Olympic experience?
Hermannová: Since the procedure from the group was our goal, on the one hand it is fine, good.
We handled what we had to say.
But now I'm sad after the game.
Sluková: I'm going to get some sleep from it and from tomorrow I'll only be reminiscuously of the Olympic tournament.
The big brother's here, here comes the first spruce SY SIM.
You won't escape her.
The new rules are intended to apply to all non-Thai-feeded persons who decide to purchase the SIM of one of the local operators when staying in Thailand.
Therefore, not only for tourists, but also for people with long-term visas, who, for example, work or marriage in Thailand.
Takorn Tantasith, Secretary-General of the National Broadcast and Telecommunications and Telecommunications Commission, said there would be no exceptions.
The plan for the sale of SIMs intended exclusively for foreigners was introduced to the national telecommunications regulator at the beginning of August.
A new measure, which will allow monitoring their location, intends to be implemented by the authorities within six months.
According to Tantasith, it is a means of maintaining national security, which will make it possible to deal with criminal activities committed by foreigners.
The so-called tourist SIM should also help to detect people who exceed the allowed length of stay.
Within half a year, a special SIM for foreigners should also appear at retailers within six-month-old SIM.
There will be a difference in the viewing function, which will be permanently switched on for cards designated persons without a Thai passport.
You won't be able to turn it off.
"It doesn't have this SIM feature for Thais, because we can always track them easily," Tantasith added.
It should be added that in Thailand prepayments are unwritten as in the Czech Republic.
The reseller requires proof of identity.
On the other hand, for several years, one of the operators handed out a free-to-end start-off SIM for tourists at airports.
So they were fully anonymous.
Ten telecommunicable regulators from other Asian countries, such as Malaysia and Singapore, are also considering introducing the same measure as Thailand.
There is currently a mandatory SIM registration, but it is not possible to legislate the legal use of the tracking system.
Execution, however, does not impertain foreigners an obligation to buy a local operator when entering the country.
Of course, they can use crunapping services with their own home SIM, in which case the Thai authorities will be short.
Operators of a given country know only relevant network information after crossing the boundary, i.e. after SIM logging in to their network.
Therefore, they know the phone number, but they do not information about the owner.
However, the difference in roaming prices compared to the rates of local operators is so abysmal that even the majority of tourists during their stay in Thailand begin to look for local SIMs.
Efquation of the authorities to oversaw the movement of foreigners legally received immediate criticism by foreigners living in Thailand.
Tantasith reassures the sanctuary and, in connection with the protection of privacy, compare the forthcoming system to the obligation to provide a residential address in Immigration documents.
In addition, the police will need a responsorororance, if necessary to monitor a specific person.
Undetermined punishment will be imminent for any misuse.
"While the service provider monitors the location of the SIM without an injunction, he will be charged with a crime," Tantasith added.
In addition, the system should maximize the use of prepayed cards.
While there will currently be recycling of a specific number only after the expiration of the 90-Day period, during which the SIM did not log in to the network, the number could now be restored after fifteen days.
Luxurious apartment in a new building in Vinohrady has a swimming pool and whirlpool on the terrace.
Why did you choose an apartment in a new building?
We were looking for a carefree apartment, where most of the care of the property will be taken care of by an administrator.
The condition was enough parking spaces and an elevator in the house, which basically disqualified an older development.
Not to mention problematic acoustics in old houses, problems with rocking the floor, high energetic energy intensity and the like.
The condition was also the large terraces, which the old development is not able to offer.
You can find other interesting visits in the magazine Modern apartment.
The entire interior was designed by architect Lenka Langerová with her colleague Kamila Poláková from the Artiga studio.
Why did you reach out to them?
Do you recommend cooperation with an expert to anyone?
Collaboration with an architect would be recommended to everyone if he designs an apartment from the very beginning, because it is a very complex technical process.
The second plane is the interior design itself: designers have a significantly better idea of the range of possible materials, and based on the client's wishes, they are able to match the interior appropriately.
We already cooperated with architect Lenka Langerová on our mountain apartment, then on the basis of the personal recommendation of another client, and due to the fact that everything worked well, it was a clear choice for us even during the reconstruction of our new apartment in Vinohrady.
What were your practical and aespathetic requirements?
We wanted a generous societal-residential space with an adequate background for a family of four conceived as a modern, but not cold interior.
The priority was to achieve the interconnection of the apartment with the terraces and to realize the intelligent control of the household.
And, of course, it was necessary to control the quality of the execution.
Did you leave everything to the experts, or did you yourself interfere with the final form of the interior?
We approved all designs, including technical drawings and samples of materials, but the design always arose from the architects.
At the outset, we determined what we expect from the design and showed some elements of the interior, which we would like to incorporate into the design, such as carpets or paintings.
I think it was our collection of mostly Czech authors, including the works of Karel Malich, Karel Vyschil, Václav Špála or Otto Gutfreund, the architect to understand our taste.
Also the combination of black, white and walnut wood was an architect's idea as well as individual pieces of furniture, walls, flooring, lighting, carpets, textiles, art, etc. We then verified together using visualization.
Were there any comprehension during the implementation, due to which you had to give up the original ideas, or vice versa, adjoin the new ones?
Perhaps only in the introductory phase of the project, when we came to the building at the first time it was possible to ascend to the height of our floor, and they found that the views we had planned would actually shatter the surrounding buildings.
At that time we literally changed the disposition overnight from the ground.
Granuite was used on the kitchen line and for lining the fireplace wall.
In the interior there are predominant wooden surfaces supplemented by leather, for example for upholstery camp and bar stools Norma from Arper.
How do you characterize the interior?
Are you going to make any changes or is there anything you would like to have differently today?
I think that it is a modern air interior, which primarily fulfills its purpose.
We do not plan the changes, most modifications after moving took place on the level of shading and settling of terraces.
Perhaps the only rebuke to the floor, when walnut wood proves to be too sensitive to the household with a dog.
The word of the architect, Ing. arch. Lenka Langerová and MgA. Kamila Hornychová, author of the realization.
The apartment is on the top floor of a new building, so it was possible to carry out a completely new disposition solution and several other modifications.
An important element is the placement of skylights in the ceiling of the corridor and the main toilet.
In doing so, we got the daylight into the darker areas of the apartment.
Also direct access from the main bathroom to the rooftop terrace to the pool from USSPA is one of the construction modifications, which was very beneficial for the function of the entire apartment.
There is also a swimming pool and a hot tub.
Compared to the French, you are not negative, says a Muslim woman living in Prague.
You come from France.
Where have you ever lived before you moved to Prague?
My Parents are originally from Algeria.
I was born in the south of France, near the Spanish border.
Quite a lot I've moved.
I've lived in Toulouse, Lyon and Marseille.
In France I studied journalism, thanks to which I then went to work in a magazine.
Then why did you decide to move out of the country?
I began to feel like I was suffocated in France.
I just didn't want to live there anymore.
Mainly because of the atmosphere among people.
It seemed to me that every Frenchman is terribly negative.
The people of France have been a negative mood in general, but it's gotten even worse lately lately.
It is interesting that you mention negativism.
In this way, some people value the Czech character.
Believe me, you're not negative compared to the French.
It may not come this way for some when they come to France because they don’t perceive it as much as tourists.
But for me it was too much.
A big theme, for example, is money.
People have the feeling that they have little money, yet the minimum wage in France is fifteen hundred euros, which I think is enough.
In addition, now there is a problem with people's behavior towards Muslims.
That's completely incomprehensible to me.
For example, my non-Muslim neighbor began to look at me in the street in which I was a non-Muslim only because I'm a Muslim.
It's like I'm some kind of an evil.
That's why I didn't feel comfortable in France.
But my family stayed there.
So you've decided to go to another country.
Why did you choose the Czech Republic?
I was looking for a job somewhere else and I came across a vacancy with Air France here in Prague.
Admittedly, I didn’t even know Prague at first.
I knew something about the Czech Republic, my father even told me about what was going on in Czechoslovakia, but I didn’t even know what to expect.
I've been here for three years and I can'n't imagine I wouldn't live here.
Did it make a big difference when you moved from France to Prague?
Mainly due to a completely different atmosphere.
Maybe it's because I don't speak Czech, so I can't capture any negative reactions or insults, but I feel like there are people more free.
That I can do anything, to get dressed in any way, and no one's going to judge me.
In France, there is always annoying looking at you, for example, when you have special clothes and the like.
I started to feel really free in Prague.
I don't think Praguers take care of such things.
Where do you work now?
After a job at Air France, I worked for a travel site for a while.
Then we and a friend and I set up a website on which people offer and are looking for shared housing.
So far we operate only in Prague, but we would like to expand it to the whole of Central Europe.
Do you have any Czech friends in Prague?
I have two very close-familiar friends here who are the Czechs from the middle, and two more friends of the Czech.
I have to say that there is another specificity of the Czechs is that at first they keep their distance.
They don't let anyone near their bodies.
That's quite a difference compared to the place where I grew up.
In the south, everyone hugs each other and kisses on the cheek.
It's different here, but I understand it's a cultural issue, so I'm trying to adapt.
You mentioned that you do not speak Czech.
Is a language barrier a problem for you?
On the contrary, I have it quite easy in Prague, because perhaps everyone here speaks English.
Even in curfecks or food.
I'd like to learn Czech, but I don't have so much time.
And by the fact that everyone can communicate at least a little bit in English, I don’t have such a need to learn the language quickly.
And every time I've got a problem, there's someone to help.
Have you encountered any negative reactions?
Maybe because you're a Muslim?
Basically, I don't.
Maybe it's also because I'm working in an environment that's more tolerant.
There's one experience I'm thinking.
My leader, the Czech woman, once told me about the French football team and uttered something in the sense of: After all, they are not French, but Africans.
It kind of touched me.
But that someone would have a problem with being a Muslim, it hasn't happened to me yet.
Even though a colleague once alerted me to some anti-Muslim demonstrations.
He told me not to go downstairs because they'd be chasing.
Anti-Muslim Demonstrations are partly related to terrorist attacks.
How does the current situation affect you?
Aren't you afraid that something similar to Paris, Nice or Brussels can also happen in Prague?
I feel safe in Prague.
But the problem is that anything can happen anywhere these days.
Until recently, I couldn't have imagined anything like this had happened in France.
And it doesn't just apply to Muslims.
Islam doesn’t recognize violence at all.
For me, people who kill aren't Muslims.
The Qur'an says that man does not have the right to kill because there is no God.
How do you feel when terrorists shield themselves with your faith?
It's very hard.
I feel shame and guilt, even though I shouldn’t feel that way.
I'm trying not to follow those reports of violence too much, but every time I'm going to read something, I feel the need to apologise for something that isn't really my fault.
I'm a completely different person, so why should I apologise for terrorists?
We went down to Jiřího z Poděbrady Square.
On Wednesday, Czech Muslims, together with Christians, were demonstrators against violence.
Have you registered it?
I didn't know.
But I understand that these people feel the need to prove that they don't agree with violence.
I don’t think they have to do something like that.
So why should they feel blame for something they didn't cause?
Do you meet other Muslims in Prague?
My collaborators, for example, are from Egypt and other Muslim countries.
They're a little more religious than I do, so we sometimes have quite savage discussions about Islam, because we all perceive it a little differently.
I come from France, so I went through a different education, for example, they taught Islam at school.
We have different approaches, but we respect each other.
People sometimes associate Islam with the subordinate status of a woman.
How do you see it?
There's no subordinate in any way. I don't feel subordinate.
Rather on the contrary.
I'm a woman, I've got strength.
It is also related to the fact that you do not wear a headgear.
Hijabs, for example?
This is mainly influenced by the way I have been brought up.
My mussband, for example, is a really principled Muslim.
It prays five times a day, but the headscarf just doesn’t.
She has always taught me that faith is a purely personal thing.
It's up to everyone how they misinterpret it.
When you read the Qur'an, you should make your own mind about these things.
So when I read a passage in Koran that says that God told the Prophet to hide his wives from the eyes of others, I said to myself, “I’m not the Prophet’s wife, so why should I hide?
On the other hand, I respect it, and when I come to Iran or Saudi Arabia, where scarves they require, I take it.
Do you plan to stay in Prague?
For now, yes.
I didn't feel so good anywhere else.
For a while I was thinking about Poland, but in Prague I feel great.
I can even imagine raising my own children here.
In my opinion, Prague is an ideal city.
Czech Muslims, together with the Christians, demonstrated against violence.
