For many centuries the village of Inveraray has been the regional center and seat of the Duke of Argyll.
From the middle of the 18-th century there have been courts at the town hall and the basement under the arrondissement served as a local prison.
The conditions in which convicts dwelt were pituitable and many prisoners attempted to escape.
A lot of them even successfully.
That's why city representatives have decided to move the prison premises to a new location.
The new building was completed in 1848 and was much friendlier to the inmates, it had heating and gas lighting.
Either way, it has not been in operation for a long time, due to economic reasons it already took place at the end of August 1889, because the smaller corrective devices were costly to operate and did not pay off too much.
Taste of convicts as well as ghost hunts.
Prison opened to the public a century later, visitors can compare an old building with a new one to see for themselves how different the conditions prevailed.
Conductors in contemporary costumes amplify an authentic experience.
You can also see the torture room, in which visitors can find out what forms of punishment were applied to criminals, whether it was breaking fingers or firing signs on the body.
As part of the tour, tourists can be locked up in one of the cells, usually because of a photograph in remembrance, or to evoke the feeling that prisoners experience behind bars.
In addition, Inveraray Prison is one of Scotland's best-known localities where it allegedly haunts.
There have already been several groups of paranormal activity hunters and to this day the museum allows people to come to hunt for ghosts.
