Army officers misrepresent intelligence about IS, says parliamentary committee
In a report issued on Thursday, the parliamentary committee reported that officers from the U.S. Central Command changed intelligence reports to portray a more optimistic picture of a war against the Islamic State in Iraq and in Syria than provided by events from the country.
According to a pre-trial report of a working group compiled by the Republican Chairman of the Committee on Armed Forces, the Intelligence Committee and the Subcommittee on Defense, "Extensive dissatisfaction" was found among central intelligence analysts, according to which their superiors have changed their assessment of American efforts to defeat the Islamic State.
The Central Headquarters, known as CentCom, is an army-central in Tampa, Florida that oversaw U.S U.S. military surgeries in the Middle East and Central Asia.
“The outcomes of the intelligence services approved by Centcom’s superior officers usually offered a more positive picture of US counter-treatory efforts than those confirmed by facts on the ground and were consistently more positive than analyses obtained from other parts of the intelligence community.”
"What's Happened in Centcom is unacceptable - our fighters are aggrieved when an erroneous analysis is presented to senior policy makers," Ken Calvert, congresswoman and Republican from California, said in a statement.
Failure in the leadership of Centcom extends to the pinnacle of the organization.
The ten-page report describes in detail the lingering problems of the central staff when describing and analysing U.S. Efstrictions initiating Iraqi forces training in 2014 and 2015.
Although there is no decisive evidence that the leaders of the Obama administration have inclined to make the reports be alternated, the report said that analysts had the impression they were under pressure from Centcom leaders to present a more optimistic view of the danger posed by the Islamic State, or IS.
"In the first half of 2015, there were a number of press releases of the Central Staff, statements and statements from the Congress considerably more positive than what the events actually were," the report said.
For example, a Centcom official has publicly stated that a major military attack to take over Mosul could begin as early as April or May 2015.
Mosul, Iran's second-largest city, remains controlled by the Islamic State.
"This is quite clear after months of investigation," Mike Pompeo, a representative of the Republican Party from Kansas, said in a statement.
From mid-2014 to mid-2015, U.S. General Staff intelligence personnel were manipulated with the outings of staff's intelligence services in order to downprehensive the dangers of IS in Iraq.
The Republicans formed a working group after they learned about analysts' fears that intelligence information related to the Islamic State was being manipulated.
The report published on Thursday must be followed by a more detailed examination after further investigation.
A further investigation is being carried out in Centcom's intelligence service, led by the Inspector General of the Ministry of Defence.
Democratic members of the Intelligence Committee published their own findings on Thursday, which corresponded to some of the Republican working group's conclusions.
"By 2014 and 2015, Centcom has created a very close process for compiling an assessment of information on ISs and Iraqi security forces," Adam B. Schiff, Chairman of the Committee and Deputy Demolitions, said in a statement in a statement.
According to Adam Schiff, the process “leaded to distort the outputs of the intelligence services,” he harmed the morale among analysts and “unsufficiently took into account dissenting views.”
Nevertheless, Schiff and Democrats say they have found no evidence that the White House was attempting to pressure Centcom's analysts to adjust their conclusions according to a "proposal or the political situation."
Preliminary results of the parliamentary investigation were published in the Daily Beast on Tuesday.
The New York Times in August reported an ongoing Pentagon inspector-general investigation, which was launched after complaints by Centcom analysts.
Commander-commander-member Patrick Evans, a Pentagon spokeswoman, stated in a statement that the Defense Ministry will not comment on the parliamentary report, as the inspector-gener's investigation is still ongoing.
“Experts sometimes don’t co-consider the interpretation of comprehensive data, and that the intelligence community and the Ministry of Defense welcome a healthy dialogue about these crucial issues of national security.”
Investigations have repercussions beyond the question of whether U.S.-led bombing in Iraq and Syria is successful (an elaboration now claimed by an increasing number of defense officials).
However last year's accusations provoked question marks on the extent to which President Obama can rely on sincerely assessing military operations in Iraq, Afghanistan, Lybia and other crisis venues.
Criticism of intelligence over the Islamic State has falsified conflicts for more than a decade since Centcom intelligence analyst Gregory Hooker wrote a research work stating that the administration of President George W. W. Bush - in spite of the objections of many analysts - defended a small unit in Iraq and devoted little more time to thinking about what's going to happen next in the 2003 invasion.
Hooker also played a key role in opposition to Islamic State's intelligence services.
Last year the authorities announced that Hooker's team had come to the conclusion that, despite public statements of the opposite, airstrike strikes against Islamic State refinery did not debilit the terrorist group's financial resources have weakened, as the group has built temporary refineries and sells oil on the black market.
The Times reported in September that the findings had not been handed over outside the headquarters.
The commander of Centcom, Gen. Lloyd J. Austin III, was then criticized last year after many legislators found the testimony of the senate very posing a war.
Resume Austin retired in April and was retired in Centcom by Gen. Joseph L. Votel.
