Adblock Plus - Facebook in cat and mice game to block ads
Attempts to circumnavigate advertising-blocking programs have become a cat-and-mouse game for companies that make money on online marketing. At least that's what one of the companies that program such software says.
On Thursday, only two days after Facebook has announced that it will block advertising filters on its desktop site, Adblock Plus published a post on its site stating that users can update the filters in their program and re-block advertising on this social network.
Facebook on Tuesday reported that its desktop website will change the encoding around advertisements to make it more difficult for advertising filters to find out which parts of the page are advertising.
Adblock now maintains that they have already circumnavigated the new codes.
Adblock Spokeswoman Ben Williams wrote in a blog post that "this kind of war between an open-source ad-blocking community and trying to circumnavigate them has persisted ever since blocking ads was invented," and that Facebook is likely to come up with another self-repair.
The fact that a company owning the world's largest social network has engaged in the war only proves the big problem blocking ads for the industry has become.
According to the study, Pagefair carried out last year, and Adobe is used by around $200 million users around the world - nearly quadruple than it did in 2013.
“Mobile advertising now generates 84% of Facebook’s ad profits, with 1.03-bli Billificoed users from mobile devices every day.
The spokeswoman said the company goes against advertising filters on the desktops, because the mobile filters of ads for mobile applications for the social network and Instagram do not work.
Advertisements on the desktops still represent a profit high enough that Facebook decides to invest in it.
Facebook also announced on Tuesday that it will update the "ad preference settings tool) to give people the opportunity to better control how their ads are displayed.
On Thursday, Facebook stated that Adblock's new filters are troubling them because they could also block content unresponsored on the site.
“It is not a pleasant experience for the people and we want to solve this problem,” a spokeswoman said in an e-mail statement.
Advertising blocking is an insensitive tool, and that’s why we’d rather focus on building tools, such as Ad Preferences, which will give control to people.
