Tolerant city.
Prague is a popular destination for the LGBT community.
Association of Czech Travel Agencies and Agencies (AČCKA) expects up to up to 20 thousand visitors to come to Prague Pride.
Kateřina Petříčková, Managing Director of AČCKA, said this on Friday.
Prague Pride’s sixth year began on August 8, the festival brings the life of lesbian, gay, bi-Gypsum and transgender (LGBT).
Approximately 35 thousand people attended the event last year.
"Prague is one of the most popular destinations of the LGBT community, due to a high rate of tolerance and a wide range of specialized businesses," Petříčková said.
According to a survey of organizers, foreign festival visitors spend an on average 3,100 kroner per person a day.
Gay tourists are available to support their travel special maps of Prague with a complete list of Prague gay and gay-friendly bars, club clubs, cafes and restaurants in the form of mobile applications, etc.
"For tourism in general, LGBT clients are very lucrative, they are interested in stays in higher price relations with a high standard of travel services and spends more than others," Petříčková said.
As part of the festival's program, the first Pride Tourism Symposium takes place this year.
Experts from the LGBT tourism sector from Europe, the U.S. and China give a lecture.
The festival continues until Sunday, August 14.
The main theme this this year is love, but the event will also be devoted to several thematic areas, which are family, AIDS prevention and trans-gender issues.
The culmination of the festival on Saturday will be a traditional parade with allegorical cars, which will come from Wenceslas Square to Letná.
The procession, which has been attended by thousands of people in recent years this year, this year with a minute's silence will commemorate the victims of the June nightclub attack in Orland, where an assailant shot dead 49 people, mainly homosexuals.
Pat and Mat celebrate for forty years.
Irresistible DIYers Pat and Mat, whose efforts usually end in catastrophe, have entertained both small and large spectators for for forty years.
The pilot of this evening series directed by Lubomír Beneš premiered on August 12, 1976.
Behind the emergence of this animated series, which is now known to viewers in more than a hundred countries of the world, were cartoon jokes of the artist and director Lubomír Beneš, which he was inventing for a magazine with two handite lords Ouholíček and Sedlec.
Beneš then invited dramaturge Jiří Kubicek and artist Vladimír Jiberek to cooperate and jointly created the film Kut'áci.
