NASA's climate model: Venus used to be habitable
According to NASA's new climatic model, Venus used to be habitable.
Researchers at Goddard's innay NASA's innay study of the universe have evolved a model to explore Venus using tools and algorithms similar to those used by climate-study scientists.
The model has been designed to determine whether Venushi, whose surface is hot without water and saturated with CO2, conditions could have been similar to conditions on Earth.
Scientists have previously suggested that the low velocity vetting of Venus can be explained by its dense atmosphere.
According to the latest simulations, it seems that Venus could once pride herself on a saline atmosphere similar to the Earth, while rotating slowly.
The model also dealt with an earlier Topography of Venus.
On the planet there were probably drought-fished areas than on Earth, but there was once plenty of water and geographical diversity on it to dominate life and provide protection against rapid evaporation.
Vemus also formerly took advantage of the sun, which was 30 percent muffled.
"In a simulation of the GISS Venus during the slow rotation, he exposes his side to the sun for almost two months at a time at the same time," GISS scientist Anthony Del Genio explained in a new report.
In this way there is the heating of the surface and the formation of rain, which creates a dense layer of clouds, which behave like an umbrella and shield the surface from excessive solar heat.
As a result, a climistry with mediocious temperatures, which are actually slightly lower than currently on Earth.
The model has compiled the story of Venus similar to an evolution previously hypoteniously compiled by planetary scientists.
Even though Venus was probably habitable in the past -- over billions of years -- has been subjected to too much heat and ultraviolet ray.
Her evaporators evaporated and water vapors were burnt with ultraviolet light.
Hydrogen has escaped, leaving only the densile atmosphere of carbon dioxide with the deadly effect of the greenhouse gas -- it's Venus as we know it today.
The researchers published their results from the new Venus climate model in the journal Geophysical Research Letters.
