Workers on oil rig in the North Sea rebuild the talks and cut off the strike
The heads-and-secret parties have said that strikes over wages for oil rig workers in the North Seas are interrupted, while all sides of the dispute have come together for a new round of negotiations.
There have been sabotage strikes against the Wood Group oil service society sporadically occurred on platforms in the North Sea, operated by Royal Dutch Shell.
"Wood Group and trade union Unite and RMT can confirm that they have agreed to relaunch a process for resolving the current non-conflict," it said in a statement.
Starting next week, over the next few weeks, we will have a number of all-day meetings, where we will try to look at the problems from new angles.
According to the trade unions, their recent action, which according to their words was the first in more than 25 years, is in protest against wage cuts of up to 30 per cent.
Wood Group lowered rates this year, paying off about 9 per cent less suppliers in the North Sea due to negative market pressure.
Company rate hikes comes after a 10 per cent reduction for offshore companies in 2014.
The company maintains that further cuts would provide companies with room to cope with a market downturn.
On the other hand, Shell strangled thousands from its payroll and announced plans to abandon oil-fuel and gas operations in up to 10 countries due to the modernization of its processes - immediately after a mega fusion with the British energy company BG Group.
Energy companies are under pressure due to low crude oil prices, the economic situation associated with Britain's June decision to leave the European Union.
Scozcocland parliament member Dave Anderson met with trade union leaders earlier this week to discuss one of the biggest working disagreements of the entire generation that led to a North Sea strike.
The North Sea, as he said, is vitally important to the economy in the region.
"In the North Sea and across the country, we need an economy that works for everyone, not just for some," he said in a statement.
For comparison, in 2016, nearly $1.4 billion is projected to be invested in new projects in the North Sea, while the average over the past five years was around $7 billion.
Kepler measures the speed of rotation of stars in Pleiades
Kepler's latest space telescope mission to help astronomers is to get a near-to-shoume list at the speed of rotating stars from the Pleiady star cluster.
New figures help astronomers to understand why stars in the star cluster rotate at different velocity, as well as how the rotation time relates to the formation of the planet.
"We hope that by comradifying our results with other star clusters, we will learn more about the relationship between stellar matter, its age and even history in its solar system," Luisa Rebull, a researcher at Caltech's Infrared Processing and Analytical Center at the Pasadena, said in a new report.
Pleiades are located 445 light-years from the Earth.
A lot of stars in a clench right now are reaching an age of adulthood and have originated for about 125 million years.
Now he's rotating the quickest.
Moments of maximum rotation speeds, these teenage stars release huge amounts of stellar wind.
Because these winds move along the magnetic fields of stars, they have a decelerating effect.
As the star is angaunted, its speed of rotation also slows down.
Kepler measures the velocity of each star by measuring the time between discovering distinguishable dots on stars -- similar to sunspots.
According to the latest data on rotation speeds, larger stars rotate more slowly than less massive stars.
Rebull compares the stars to the dancers, the larger they make pirouettes more slowly than the smaller ones.
“We can’t see that slower-tempering stars are more massive, while those spinning the fastest are usually very light-emitting stars,” Rebull said.
As the Pleiades-star cluster serves as an ideal cossembly laboratory to compare star-study and rotate speed.
“Polishers provide us with an anchor for theoretical models of stellar rotation in both directions, in younger and older stars,” Rebull said.
There's still a lot we want to find out about how, when and why the stars are slowing down their rotation speed, so to speak "suggesting their dancing shoes on a clove".
Rebullová and her colleagues described in detail the most recent analysis of the velocity of Pleiades in three studies, which will soon be published in the Astronomical Journal.
Is a Qualification Agreement Required?
If I didn't come forward, then the qualifying agreement wouldn't be concluded and after a few months I could switch to another hospital?
Is it even possible after graduation without enrolling in specialized education?
My concern is that in the field where I would like to work there is now a vacancy only in a somewhat distant hospital, but in about half a year it could free up space in another, where then I would like to work.
Therefore, I don’t know how to deal with the situation about half a year, the question continues.
Responsible Mgr. Lenka Vlková.
There is no legal obligation to conclude a qualification agreement with the employer - a hospital.
Qualification agreement is a legal institute, which is regulated by the Labour Code, and can be concluded between the employee and the employer in order to ensure the return on the costs incurred in increasing or deepening the employee's qualifications.
At the same time, long-term pressure is being exerted in the health sector in the field of health care, disrespecting non-companiment with regard to ensuring the labour mobility of healthcare professionals, and not all hospitals currently require this agreement.
Pursuant to Act No. 95/2004 Coll., on the conditions for acquiring and recognizing profesibilities and specialized competence to perform the medical profession of a doctor, a dentist and a pharmacist, it expressly applies that participation in specialized education is considered a deepening of qualifications (not for increasing it).
This means that the qualifying agreement can be concluded according to the Labor Code only if the costs amount to at least 75 000 CZK.
There is no legal regulation or obligation to enroll in specialized education.
The law only lays down the conditions of an application for inclusion in the field of specialized education.
However, according to the law, the doctor is entitled to exercise the profession of a doctor already thanks to the necessary university studies, albeit under the legally stipulated conditions (under the professional supervision of a doctor with specialized competence in the relevant field).
The law foresees a situation where professional experience completed in another field of specialization can be counted in the specialized education of a doctor, if its content and scope correspond to the relevant educational program.
Theoretically it would thus be possible to calculate the time worked elsewhere, which would correspond to the interviewer's situation.
However, due to the fact that I do not know the detailed circumstances of this case, it can be recommended to consult with the competent department of the Ministry of Health (Union of Science and Medical Professions), as the Ministry of Health by law provides an advisory and advisory service related to inclusion in specialization education.
Consultative service is also provided free of charge by the Czech Medical Chamber, of which every doctor must be a member.
Physicians from Halab, Syria, are requesting U.S. intervening.
